)(6/4)(8/6)=4. Positivity gives E=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-and-cancel","label":"Square and cancel","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-cancel":["cnm_173","cnm_147","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_147","math:telescoping"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 10/81","reason":"For S_N=sum from n=1 to N of n/10^n, shift-subtraction gives (9/10)S_N=(1/9)(1-10^-N)-N/10^(N+1). Both remainder terms tend to zero, so S=10/81.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_018"],"id":"shift-subtract-partial-sums","label":"Shift and subtract finite partial sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-subtract-partial-sums":["math:telescoping","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:telescoping","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h207","cnm_018"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, {4,5,6,7,8}","reason":"Twice P(2) is 2log₁₀(3/2) = log₁₀(9/4). The probability of digits 4 through 8 is log₁₀9 - log₁₀4 = log₁₀(9/4), as required.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"telescope-consecutive-digit-probabilities","label":"Cancel the intermediate logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"telescope-consecutive-digit-probabilities":["cnm_h138","cnm_h044","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h138","cnm_h044","math:telescoping","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 1/10","reason":"The product is (1/2)(2/3)(3/4)···(9/10). All internal factors cancel, leaving